186045417] [17592186543327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543327 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543327 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543327 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543327 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "37ff7102-c223-52be-869b-375cd2da2fab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543404 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543404 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543404 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543404 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186543159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543404 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a12b2c7e-438f-5baa-bede-06e74205a643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543438 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c39a56b0-17ca-5ba8-97f8-8c9ae43975ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543443 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cbcf9c63-b025-5bf8-b0d4-e2ed9ac04b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543463 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f0da2e57-6d30-583d-ba2b-acd1651fbc57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543485 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $-3,\\,33,\\,-363,\\,…$\n\nII. $4,\\,52,\\,572,\\,…$\n\nIII. $-2,\\,24,\\,-312,\\,…$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28e7f38a-8ff9-507f-8c27-7cd729b83484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543485 #edb/ref 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{33}{(-3)}=-11 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-363)}{33}=-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{52}{4}=13 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{572}{52}=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{24}{(-2)}=-12 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-312)}{24}=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I could be a geometric sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543546 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n-4,\\,16,\\,-64,\\,256,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe fifth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abfcac51-46f8-5836-b296-e3921e8850cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543546 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/84/84c714b0c37c7f28cb83c59e3455755470ff6aa69da145c80ccea19200b1ad8f.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{16}{(-4)}=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $-4.$ \n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $5$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/40/400ef822bd63631f65a4190f8367e84878c03a302c3202a112e9de2858ae4249.png)\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is $-1\\,024.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543553 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,10,\\,-20,\\,…$\n\nIII. $1,\\,-4,\\,-16,\\,…$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b85423f1-a316-59dd-932f-ac0693226903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543553 #edb/ref 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{10}{4}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{(-5)}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-20)}{10}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-4)}{1}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-16)}{(-4)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II could be a geometric sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543564 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $8$, $2x$, and $x$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9c0d129-478a-533e-ada4-db9e83c26531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543564 #edb/ref 1074 "The first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=8,\\,{a}_{2}=2x,\\,{a}_{3}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2x}{8}=\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{x}{2x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543576 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543576 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543576 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543576 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\frac{x}{4}$, $x$, and $64$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543576 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6d790e0-5c33-5dec-949d-78146efef13a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543576 #edb/ref 1074 "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=\\frac{x}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=x,\\,{a}_{3}=64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{(\\frac{x}{4})}=x\\cdot \\frac{4}{x}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{64}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{64}{x} \\\\ 4x & =64 \\\\ x & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543594 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186543635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543594 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0218cccb-f086-56d9-b8f9-aa20f5c1b9cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543594 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186543485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543619 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186543623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543619 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "329da224-1a13-5b74-9041-4ca853e42fee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543619 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186543546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543623 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a941842-0fd3-56b2-9964-8308aaf327cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543623 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186543576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543635 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186543619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59f07007-7ea1-58c4-9a83-d350969d589e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186543553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186543594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8bf393cb-72f3-500f-a512-82e3760658ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186543564 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
